WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:10.370
This is an interesting vignette, because it's
kind of shocking to ask, but of course probably

00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:13.040
some students playing with designs.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:18.000
But the fellow said he walked into a room
one day, and there's a scale model of the

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:19.450
whole of Cambridge.

00:00:19.450 --> 00:00:25.370
And it's very plain that all the pieces of
wood that were red were owned by Harvard,

00:00:25.370 --> 00:00:26.891
and the ones that were blue were owned by
MIT.

00:00:26.891 --> 00:00:34.190
And he says "Bill, there's a dotted line running
down the middle of Prospect Street and River

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:38.500
Street -- red on one side and blue on the
other."

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:42.760
And so, it's like, they're planning to divide
the whole city up!

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:49.050
But it was probably some design school student
doing his project.

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:53.750
But still, it's the kind of thing that people
always think.

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:58.960
People are always accused of being paranoid
about the power of these institutions, but

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:06.430
you know, there's hardly ... I could probably
count on the fingers of one hand the number

00:01:06.430 --> 00:01:11.000
of businesses in Central Square that are still
here that were there when I came.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:15.460
And all the factories and businesses that
were here, they're all gone.

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:20.640
But Harvard and MIT are still here, and still
growing.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:26.830
No matter who runs them or who comes and goes,
the institutions live on -- like the Commonwealth

00:01:26.830 --> 00:01:29.020
lives on, we pass as shadows.

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:38.991
A bad old joke I used to use often, "if it
weren't for Harvard and MIT, Cambridge would

00:01:38.991 --> 00:01:45.270
just be another older, urban, northeastern
city on a river."

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:54.720
It's clear that MIT and Harvard's presence
have enormous impacts on the city -- positive

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:55.720
impacts.

00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:58.710
There are very few negatives.

00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:03.040
Are there demands on the housing market by
some of the graduate students?

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:05.980
Yes, but that's going to happen in any college
town.

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:10.729
If you're going to be a college town and deal
with that issue, let the colleges be Harvard

00:02:10.729 --> 00:02:13.030
and MIT.

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:18.700
Cambridge is Cambridge in good part because
of institutions like MIT and Harvard.

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:23.670
And one hundred years of MIT, obviously just
look at Kendall Square.

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:27.460
Kendall Square has grown because of the MIT
connection and influence.

00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:33.530
It has grown in a big way, it has provided
jobs, it's provided great financial resources

00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:35.170
to the community.

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:37.650
It's provided quality construction.

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:43.400
It's now addressing issues that are at the
heart of a lot of neighborhood concerns.

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:50.099
So, I think it's provided educational opportunities
for people all around the world, as well as

00:02:50.099 --> 00:02:51.790
many Cambridge residents.

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:56.910
It's a center of research, it's a center of
ideas.

00:02:56.910 --> 00:03:01.680
Cambridge wouldn't be Cambridge without an
MIT.

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:06.750
I'm not saying that in any way other than
to say that's the truth.

00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:12.190
That we are lucky as a community to have MIT,
and I think MIT is lucky as an institution

00:03:12.190 --> 00:03:18.950
to be situated in a city like Cambridge that
thinks about the management of its city and

00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:22.879
the welfare of its residents in a way that
will challenge the university.

